</div>
<p class="ltx_p">Also, when <math class="ltx_Math" altimg="m16.png" altimg-height="18px" altimg-valign="-2px" altimg-width="18px" alttext="\mathbb{L}" display="inline"><mi href="./23.1#t1.r1" title="lattice">𝕃</mi></math> is replaced by <math class="ltx_Math" altimg="m35.png" altimg-height="18px" altimg-valign="-2px" altimg-width="27px" alttext="c\mathbb{L}" display="inline"><mrow><mi href="./23.10#iv.p1" title="constant">c</mi><mo>⁢</mo><mi href="./23.1#t1.r1" title="lattice">𝕃</mi></mrow></math> the lattice invariants <math class="ltx_Math" altimg="m42.png" altimg-height="17px" altimg-valign="-6px" altimg-width="24px" alttext="g_{2}" display="inline"><mrow><msub><mi href="./23.3#EGx1" title="Weierstrass lattice invariants 𝑔₂⁡, 𝑔₃⁡">g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo></mrow></math>
and <math class="ltx_Math" altimg="m43.png" altimg-height="17px" altimg-valign="-6px" altimg-width="24px" alttext="g_{3}" display="inline"><mrow><msub><mi href="./23.3#EGx1" title="Weierstrass lattice invariants 𝑔₂⁡, 𝑔₃⁡">g</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo></mrow></math> are divided by <math class="ltx_Math" altimg="m36.png" altimg-height="21px" altimg-valign="-2px" altimg-width="23px" alttext="c^{4}" display="inline"><msup><mi href="./23.10#iv.p1" title="constant">c</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></math> and <math class="ltx_Math" altimg="m37.png" altimg-height="21px" altimg-valign="-2px" altimg-width="23px" alttext="c^{6}" display="inline"><msup><mi href="./23.10#iv.p1" title="constant">c</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="iv.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p class="ltx_p">For these results and further identities see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Lawden (<a href="./bib/L#bib1385" title="Elliptic Functions and Applications" class="ltx_ref">1989</a>, §6.6)</cite> and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet">Apostol (<a href="./bib/#bib119" title="Modular Functions and Dirichlet Series in Number Theory" class="ltx_ref">1990</a>, p. 14)</cite>.</p>
</div>
